{
  "id": "metacontrast",
  "term": "metacontrast",
  "definition": "Metacontrast is a kind of backward visual masking in which a briefly shown target becomes harder to see, or may disappear from awareness, when a following mask appears without overlapping it in space. Its masking effect usually follows a Type B pattern, with the strongest disruption at intermediate delays between target and mask, while very short delays can sometimes improve visibility if the two stimuli combine into a coherent perceptual whole.",
